[{:db/id "q-351304/field-1/473f36def7c891a510b40fec288aebb8efc7d39229fe13e1cfe822cc74e4d9ff" :answer/id #uuid "284ce44f-f29d-5759-9023-2377fb86bb8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351304/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a416e4d3-0e68-579c-9de3-953f68f7d461" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351304/field-1/473f36def7c891a510b40fec288aebb8efc7d39229fe13e1cfe822cc74e4d9ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-351304/field-1/473f36def7c891a510b40fec288aebb8efc7d39229fe13e1cfe822cc74e4d9ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351304" :question/id #uuid "b9628bde-1d91-5e41-8040-1238c3e3d6a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-351304" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/3}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/3}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/3}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{3}/3}^{a} \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})) \\\\  & =8\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/28afd0a065525cd225377165c2164a1ecc254b1be8aba60d64324d14430c3be4.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/3}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-351304/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aee9bcc-2c53-54d9-a3d1-3262c939f5ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351304"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-152090/selection/35d0af53e25a7911f6d08c6d063fcf9231a1273a16a0f7056530d6f5d5dfbdd8" :answer/id #uuid "dcd854d5-3cf2-576d-a1b0-3d530d535aa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152090/selection/e0bd991ac2e2f2a23ec920e03c6d5103be6b20c12a25d724ab290d4ba5292c3e" :answer/id #uuid "7c1f3e40-596d-5d0c-b691-53f7531d2675" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152090/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" :answer/id #uuid "a207b257-acda-5649-9849-d8853f14a05c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152090/selection/1a2c3881891ab7eca8aa4af8d7002d84b8252f633df18da1be453e64ebd8a8b4" :answer/id #uuid "e26212df-58db-5765-aefc-781ff2e3567d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152090/selection/655610c33f9ee70bd034b89a5aa2c6ed9461fb56dcb4fb9b8027be65bbde5b10" :answer/id #uuid "304b6e9b-24aa-5e6b-a7e7-f4cca02058e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152090/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e3b69e4-24af-50c0-8513-48f3af77f9a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-152090/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" :answer-field/choices ["q-152090/selection/35d0af53e25a7911f6d08c6d063fcf9231a1273a16a0f7056530d6f5d5dfbdd8" "q-152090/selection/e0bd991ac2e2f2a23ec920e03c6d5103be6b20c12a25d724ab290d4ba5292c3e" "q-152090/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" "q-152090/selection/1a2c3881891ab7eca8aa4af8d7002d84b8252f633df18da1be453e64ebd8a8b4" "q-152090/selection/655610c33f9ee70bd034b89a5aa2c6ed9461fb56dcb4fb9b8027be65bbde5b10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152090" :question/id #uuid "ac1690dd-eaaf-507f-94f3-3a9dfee88c31" :question/math-academy-id "q-152090" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{8}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{8}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)]_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)])\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{8}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-152090/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aee9bcc-2c53-54d9-a3d1-3262c939f5ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152090"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-152081/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" :answer/id #uuid "142f1b67-4027-5b8a-9fdc-bbfaee1cd772" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152081/selection/8b0da25323387be7cd3dda381ee4e2ebbdc8b10be241bc2e3eeead0da24f888a" :answer/id #uuid "c28c9654-e208-562c-92a1-6392d740d540" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152081/selection/b9e37961d17591920349894dfae8783436f581130d0da0bd042142640bfd4f67" :answer/id #uuid "4e086c7b-b15f-5e0e-b27e-3844fc26351e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152081/selection/f0f45940ed5df71758de7ff31ea171bc0689f380be113b3538530f3180b5105f" :answer/id #uuid "aadca629-4aa6-57b3-a4d7-8d07679b4860" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152081/selection/03864569254141960db19b242c7f1e850d6147657a3c76f443bd0fefd8fede2f" :answer/id #uuid "2ee1de72-e0de-5c4c-8deb-fb2d73ed96d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152081/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a640a830-81a9-5899-bfb9-78e7899ffc34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-152081/selection/f0f45940ed5df71758de7ff31ea171bc0689f380be113b3538530f3180b5105f" :answer-field/choices ["q-152081/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" "q-152081/selection/8b0da25323387be7cd3dda381ee4e2ebbdc8b10be241bc2e3eeead0da24f888a" "q-152081/selection/b9e37961d17591920349894dfae8783436f581130d0da0bd042142640bfd4f67" "q-152081/selection/f0f45940ed5df71758de7ff31ea171bc0689f380be113b3538530f3180b5105f" "q-152081/selection/03864569254141960db19b242c7f1e850d6147657a3c76f443bd0fefd8fede2f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152081" :question/id #uuid "2073f085-b85c-5038-8e89-9fee93e9cd0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-152081" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & =10\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-152081/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aee9bcc-2c53-54d9-a3d1-3262c939f5ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152081"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-152083/selection/1a2c3881891ab7eca8aa4af8d7002d84b8252f633df18da1be453e64ebd8a8b4" :answer/id #uuid "f8d60d0f-b482-5bc4-aa7e-82e8a001167f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152083/selection/49038b61ea067d6dd93788348f33b8daf64ac00ab39a6be298018d209f477567" :answer/id #uuid "8f8ebc2c-fc41-58dc-b107-02b42387496f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152083/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" :answer/id #uuid "fc2ff533-7f86-5de0-be26-c13b02196cd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152083/selection/338fd6744eb7ca9587a541b0bb0183ee8b61d9b20649c5de8a38a7eb04c075d9" :answer/id #uuid "cd94dbbc-f6d4-575e-9d0d-d32519354b83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152083/selection/3c0740282279356197653ff4453018a1b44d953528360220a6c62f00a3647413" :answer/id #uuid "4cece45c-14f8-57b8-8500-a7104ad752ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152083/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cfe26bf8-7334-53d2-90b4-442376a4e7ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-152083/selection/3c0740282279356197653ff4453018a1b44d953528360220a6c62f00a3647413" :answer-field/choices ["q-152083/selection/1a2c3881891ab7eca8aa4af8d7002d84b8252f633df18da1be453e64ebd8a8b4" "q-152083/selection/49038b61ea067d6dd93788348f33b8daf64ac00ab39a6be298018d209f477567" "q-152083/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" "q-152083/selection/338fd6744eb7ca9587a541b0bb0183ee8b61d9b20649c5de8a38a7eb04c075d9" "q-152083/selection/3c0740282279356197653ff4453018a1b44d953528360220a6c62f00a3647413"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152083" :question/id #uuid "34734a25-f8e6-5474-9114-31af854ab67c" :question/math-academy-id "q-152083" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z)]_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})] \\\\  & =4\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{1+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =4\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-152083/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aee9bcc-2c53-54d9-a3d1-3262c939f5ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152083"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351303/field-1/a851861bffcfd043d1922cd72af17e5308ec86b5c577715294c23d4c796ce0e2" :answer/id #uuid "9f62d823-4b18-571b-9fb5-4c9124ddb844" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351303/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "66148d2f-5598-5c40-b514-5f76db161b37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351303/field-1/a851861bffcfd043d1922cd72af17e5308ec86b5c577715294c23d4c796ce0e2" :answer-field/choices ["q-351303/field-1/a851861bffcfd043d1922cd72af17e5308ec86b5c577715294c23d4c796ce0e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351303" :question/id #uuid "a0bbd335-1cae-519c-b920-d88e53fe6aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-351303" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =12\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =12\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/28afd0a065525cd225377165c2164a1ecc254b1be8aba60d64324d14430c3be4.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =12\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =10\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-351303/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aee9bcc-2c53-54d9-a3d1-3262c939f5ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351303"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351307/field-1/29ec2dfefa1bd3742bf5ba922b9c261dd5f2325bd8484e9153eb979dbd6eced6" :answer/id #uuid "e587477e-587c-5727-9a4d-07525e5ceccf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351307/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c49c931-dde0-526a-83a6-2e1805374b52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351307/field-1/29ec2dfefa1bd3742bf5ba922b9c261dd5f2325bd8484e9153eb979dbd6eced6" :answer-field/choices ["q-351307/field-1/29ec2dfefa1bd3742bf5ba922b9c261dd5f2325bd8484e9153eb979dbd6eced6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351307" :question/id #uuid "cba6c52f-2737-507d-b1e6-2e1166e787a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-351307" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/4daa526482964d826d5cf17a9a646de0a01059e7f886ddd1e19bcb09c4c25ee3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-351307/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0c1c13e-4e93-596a-a7de-7dd8cb073537"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351307"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-347131/field-1/28f1df34d23dbda0a39aec1f79036e7c983a2f66f821a285d3bded0d11737153" :answer/id #uuid "f958973f-a2f9-5f2b-a4f4-143fa5b92988" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-347131/field-1/ee650aa44c8186602d08d4a4b59d28c8039129f3e1a5eb6a0348db4794848930" :answer/id #uuid "c5002e58-30a8-5e42-ae85-82098948c37e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-347131/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "29f911d9-0b05-5dde-91f4-adb12fd836a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-347131/field-1/28f1df34d23dbda0a39aec1f79036e7c983a2f66f821a285d3bded0d11737153" :answer-field/choices ["q-347131/field-1/28f1df34d23dbda0a39aec1f79036e7c983a2f66f821a285d3bded0d11737153" "q-347131/field-1/ee650aa44c8186602d08d4a4b59d28c8039129f3e1a5eb6a0348db4794848930"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-347131" :question/id #uuid "82d6dc09-7459-5760-b3a5-8c280a58889e" :question/math-academy-id "q-347131" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1/2}^{a}\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1/2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(2x)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]}_{1/2}^{a} \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =9\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/2ad762128a32c119f1783b88b4d10ae6820a914689296f03eedad206e097a29d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-347131/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0c1c13e-4e93-596a-a7de-7dd8cb073537"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-347131"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-152121/selection/e0bd991ac2e2f2a23ec920e03c6d5103be6b20c12a25d724ab290d4ba5292c3e" :answer/id #uuid "2dbdf3e5-d0fd-57ea-be60-52977342d35f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152121/selection/35d0af53e25a7911f6d08c6d063fcf9231a1273a16a0f7056530d6f5d5dfbdd8" :answer/id #uuid "8d8477e9-4241-58e6-92da-8eedd58d2a2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152121/selection/49038b61ea067d6dd93788348f33b8daf64ac00ab39a6be298018d209f477567" :answer/id #uuid "7748e690-dfe8-59c2-88f6-e207ac9e446d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152121/selection/adbad78d33491a071139fe589fefda7e9ba65cf97ebbcae14d252ac90459e198" :answer/id #uuid "cb02eb77-f93f-5c3e-bb2c-f78865b63751" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152121/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" :answer/id #uuid "398036e9-df4d-5bed-8769-13fa4f415336" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152121/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b6fa3b8-9168-53dc-aa38-08368bc3e3e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-152121/selection/49038b61ea067d6dd93788348f33b8daf64ac00ab39a6be298018d209f477567" :answer-field/choices ["q-152121/selection/e0bd991ac2e2f2a23ec920e03c6d5103be6b20c12a25d724ab290d4ba5292c3e" "q-152121/selection/35d0af53e25a7911f6d08c6d063fcf9231a1273a16a0f7056530d6f5d5dfbdd8" "q-152121/selection/49038b61ea067d6dd93788348f33b8daf64ac00ab39a6be298018d209f477567" "q-152121/selection/adbad78d33491a071139fe589fefda7e9ba65cf97ebbcae14d252ac90459e198" "q-152121/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152121" :question/id #uuid "27ca21a5-5ab8-57fd-b2a0-6b75d37b577d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152121" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{6}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{6}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{1}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{1}{1+(6x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)]}_{a}^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6a)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/faa31c8261818c5c978d984a9b90a13e58794b02ff6f47e868ad6d5a06b7f71a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}\\frac{6}{1+36{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-152121/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0c1c13e-4e93-596a-a7de-7dd8cb073537"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152121"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-152124/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" :answer/id #uuid "e66ac7fa-3947-5547-99f4-866ab494c658" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152124/selection/a996cecead059c2e05b8ded2ceb60e4e2170a4344e0c20625524ed13b23c6fbe" :answer/id #uuid "b067e56c-54ff-5359-b2cb-a71d7f34b945" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152124/selection/07a3540ad51bfb53d362c69b0c3835b387a5d7d769d089dc5189f608ec57d640" :answer/id #uuid "81b75e88-4d87-5af3-bd76-de3f6ea58aa5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152124/selection/7b1b8538b264eef6681d0ab5f3b14d51b6cdff67f34fba59d0886ae5c6acf80a" :answer/id #uuid "45e1f583-0297-5323-91d4-2371c15aa46d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152124/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951" :answer/id #uuid "76a8d4c3-0a8c-5100-9c05-91b903d06e34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152124/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "38f8eeb5-5f00-5c26-880e-5511a31c40cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-152124/selection/7b1b8538b264eef6681d0ab5f3b14d51b6cdff67f34fba59d0886ae5c6acf80a" :answer-field/choices ["q-152124/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" "q-152124/selection/a996cecead059c2e05b8ded2ceb60e4e2170a4344e0c20625524ed13b23c6fbe" "q-152124/selection/07a3540ad51bfb53d362c69b0c3835b387a5d7d769d089dc5189f608ec57d640" "q-152124/selection/7b1b8538b264eef6681d0ab5f3b14d51b6cdff67f34fba59d0886ae5c6acf80a" "q-152124/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152124" :question/id #uuid "30ad665c-f55a-51ff-a915-781d4d520289" :question/math-academy-id "q-152124" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{6}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{6}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]}_{a}^{-\\sqrt{3}/2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)] \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)) \\\\  & =-\\pi -3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/382ca84f05af04af9c9b2c837d549a09b695574781f12bc67b8438b95b5f5d75.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\pi -3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)] \\\\  & =-\\pi -3\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\pi +\\frac{3\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-152124/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0c1c13e-4e93-596a-a7de-7dd8cb073537"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152124"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-347129/field-1/30c65f95461d7cf832bda679931de2b4099e743d19de1b695cb0707c6a50ede3" :answer/id #uuid "7b8fe343-4fd5-5a7d-969f-ff295fc7da2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-347129/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "180d985d-f42a-5557-a1fa-8354e5dac119" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-347129/field-1/30c65f95461d7cf832bda679931de2b4099e743d19de1b695cb0707c6a50ede3" :answer-field/choices ["q-347129/field-1/30c65f95461d7cf832bda679931de2b4099e743d19de1b695cb0707c6a50ede3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-347129" :question/id #uuid "a1d4b562-1c95-546f-bdd3-a7e305ca27a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-347129" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{(3x)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]}_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/9f882cb5102270dfe4bef256782c18aaeeca3518f564cd188298040237e30f62.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-347129/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0c1c13e-4e93-596a-a7de-7dd8cb073537"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-347129"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-152128/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" :answer/id #uuid "791039e2-75d8-56ba-b175-84229be613ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152128/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" :answer/id #uuid "407d393e-a87a-575e-8245-8f7466765b4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152128/selection/7b1b8538b264eef6681d0ab5f3b14d51b6cdff67f34fba59d0886ae5c6acf80a" :answer/id #uuid "c5c44b7c-4723-5df7-8892-863ec5351609" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152128/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951" :answer/id #uuid "f6d0a533-0f52-53c6-b403-3a92e5bc03ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152128/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" :answer/id #uuid "ce1b2e1a-97ba-5ff4-8d30-7ecb778f96e1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152128/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fdf154e6-2b5c-5cbe-bcdc-81dbc41e8f66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-152128/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" :answer-field/choices ["q-152128/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" "q-152128/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" "q-152128/selection/7b1b8538b264eef6681d0ab5f3b14d51b6cdff67f34fba59d0886ae5c6acf80a" "q-152128/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951" "q-152128/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152128" :question/id #uuid "70d25320-cdfc-5522-98e3-9eb68d3be51e" :question/math-academy-id "q-152128" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-152128/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8a8bf54-5523-5eaa-a236-b8931d1c3dba"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152128"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-6037"] :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{z}{3})]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{3})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/2465f099a94aa966dbf33eb89aa00973e692fa97c887a9b79baff8260f5d31cd.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-351568/field-1/29ec2dfefa1bd3742bf5ba922b9c261dd5f2325bd8484e9153eb979dbd6eced6" :answer/id #uuid "3232797b-ccb3-53ee-8389-2759ac8471c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351568/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1867bb57-b126-5a2a-b6c2-2e9ac5b7dcca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351568/field-1/29ec2dfefa1bd3742bf5ba922b9c261dd5f2325bd8484e9153eb979dbd6eced6" :answer-field/choices ["q-351568/field-1/29ec2dfefa1bd3742bf5ba922b9c261dd5f2325bd8484e9153eb979dbd6eced6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351568" :question/id #uuid "9a5e0d15-c254-5692-98e6-7c97cecac4f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-351568" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{z}{3})]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{3})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/a4961fa6f4f1aafeba2e8f77ed91e7d034fffd6aa249f13bb8357da11a7cea5e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\cdot (0-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{3})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-351568/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8a8bf54-5523-5eaa-a236-b8931d1c3dba"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351568"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-119199/selection/a4ac792bbed362debd7723cff583c29089245f93a1d10fee5bfd2e4c5c551a68" :answer/id #uuid "99881aa7-619e-5579-86fa-6772d06f7b4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119199/selection/adbad78d33491a071139fe589fefda7e9ba65cf97ebbcae14d252ac90459e198" :answer/id #uuid "29b77151-5e8f-5608-8812-988d3fb4063f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119199/selection/49038b61ea067d6dd93788348f33b8daf64ac00ab39a6be298018d209f477567" :answer/id #uuid "a67c773c-38a3-5823-88bf-8656b608dc6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119199/selection/9739ae9d123f99d96caea0c29a080d927663e4fd583fea327883bb6fa91dcb9e" :answer/id #uuid "59e6d469-4d73-5505-84e1-9ac37857a788" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119199/selection/ccd5f5a0bb200035d5ce986a09138fe0d8854b37aa8f5be800caaa810aa80cfc" :answer/id #uuid "75faa20e-81b8-5b85-b01c-c11c653d0586" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3\\sqrt{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-119199/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb1bca8-73e9-557f-a91e-2d99d9943901" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-119199/selection/ccd5f5a0bb200035d5ce986a09138fe0d8854b37aa8f5be800caaa810aa80cfc" :answer-field/choices ["q-119199/selection/a4ac792bbed362debd7723cff583c29089245f93a1d10fee5bfd2e4c5c551a68" "q-119199/selection/adbad78d33491a071139fe589fefda7e9ba65cf97ebbcae14d252ac90459e198" "q-119199/selection/49038b61ea067d6dd93788348f33b8daf64ac00ab39a6be298018d209f477567" "q-119199/selection/9739ae9d123f99d96caea0c29a080d927663e4fd583fea327883bb6fa91dcb9e" "q-119199/selection/ccd5f5a0bb200035d5ce986a09138fe0d8854b37aa8f5be800caaa810aa80cfc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-119199" :question/id #uuid "90a4c7ed-9667-5ba1-b600-ffa3efc42160" :question/math-academy-id "q-119199" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}})]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/11c74897fe914599a78c5997767bad83d13ddfb1e35ee66622d8880743688bad.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-119199/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8a8bf54-5523-5eaa-a236-b8931d1c3dba"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-119199"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-152132/selection/6574705814da0db6b3aa28dbb2a09d324bd280d2223ca9aa9dd5784f9d06bd93" :answer/id #uuid "15724977-de0d-59a6-b7af-ca80d559811e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152132/selection/0dbec4870e57573286a3b337231517a95e48a6378854240cef143d28f877d639" :answer/id #uuid "55c35a82-bbea-55c8-b000-ff4d02cc781e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152132/selection/d62732e72e17b0393290bd9e548ef368f6b7424bded817d038b07db5bf5fcb98" :answer/id #uuid "a63a2e03-f856-55b9-828c-16979852107f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152132/selection/1799e19c9a3a0d0b1889be2c54ab73f5730109da85155b3b8b0ccf6c4adbeaca" :answer/id #uuid "3c2bbee2-6e77-5b1b-8104-656f4530185a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152132/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" :answer/id #uuid "dbc09e01-ba3a-5367-bb7f-c19324310402" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152132/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3431ab19-9897-55d3-ad08-fb6acd7f432c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-152132/selection/6574705814da0db6b3aa28dbb2a09d324bd280d2223ca9aa9dd5784f9d06bd93" :answer-field/choices ["q-152132/selection/6574705814da0db6b3aa28dbb2a09d324bd280d2223ca9aa9dd5784f9d06bd93" "q-152132/selection/0dbec4870e57573286a3b337231517a95e48a6378854240cef143d28f877d639" "q-152132/selection/d62732e72e17b0393290bd9e548ef368f6b7424bded817d038b07db5bf5fcb98" "q-152132/selection/1799e19c9a3a0d0b1889be2c54ab73f5730109da85155b3b8b0ccf6c4adbeaca" "q-152132/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152132" :question/id #uuid "70c64f6d-786d-5347-8864-22686b5cea06" :question/math-academy-id "q-152132" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{z}{5})]}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})-\\frac{\\pi }{4}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/f8325b1ea152bd40aaa909202bfce59e621952486041c657c0e27b9192ecec03.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})-\\frac{\\pi }{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-152132/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8a8bf54-5523-5eaa-a236-b8931d1c3dba"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152132"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-152660/selection/9d08cdaaebcb50b75e0c4899a76bc48fdcf67c6a2cb8ba53dbaede2dc5354629" :answer/id #uuid "f62814d5-b1d9-5c45-82dd-bbcb75aaf2cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152660/selection/8edf36f0d0c04b8e0e3f881a66fab0f1901a06368d5ca1f6d437f77714de6bc5" :answer/id #uuid "3ad169ce-a452-525b-9cb8-d117a0a4e328" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152660/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" :answer/id #uuid "632c16de-1ffe-5c22-a682-7951f08b33fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152660/selection/9bbe1cd35c47de8280caf1f369a736289048b79bf87820fe65138c304bbd0e76" :answer/id #uuid "295cc241-1d26-5eeb-aab6-8dc6825ca6ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{24}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152660/selection/09e535b811e1e7584404784c7ac4f577a3ed9805710046fc8fb895ffb3de13a0" :answer/id #uuid "b4f3edaf-096c-5fa1-a7e6-e6345bb34543" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-152660/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb0e548b-dc3c-5450-8721-7f43f8868c1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-152660/selection/9bbe1cd35c47de8280caf1f369a736289048b79bf87820fe65138c304bbd0e76" :answer-field/choices ["q-152660/selection/9d08cdaaebcb50b75e0c4899a76bc48fdcf67c6a2cb8ba53dbaede2dc5354629" "q-152660/selection/8edf36f0d0c04b8e0e3f881a66fab0f1901a06368d5ca1f6d437f77714de6bc5" "q-152660/selection/3be4a0b33d8ae752fcff4e19cc6abeec9ea0ac2c8234b469f7c7959c2008fb10" "q-152660/selection/9bbe1cd35c47de8280caf1f369a736289048b79bf87820fe65138c304bbd0e76" "q-152660/selection/09e535b811e1e7584404784c7ac4f577a3ed9805710046fc8fb895ffb3de13a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-152660" :question/id #uuid "b6dee71c-db47-5c94-a722-97bd34eb9676" :question/math-academy-id "q-152660" :question/problem "Use the substitution $u={x}^{4}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3u)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3u)]_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/94ad95c4f0477ae2dd29538e6cfcbbd52cbd7eda5b1c29246c1b4967566a7341.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-152660/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "808894a0-c611-5eb7-bd57-7f7029f0ef0c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-152660"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351761/field-1/1dee3a8b4e2e69484264522b7c3f6207b09ecde7ad469649dd240ad6a8e6346d" :answer/id #uuid "296a3fb4-51ec-5bdc-b7ba-a9ca7606b93b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351761/field-1/608b697f811192589eec3729e28e5d056be7cd5127af1a846d717a33508ae203" :answer/id #uuid "ec3a3206-63d1-50f9-b943-79af46d4f7ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351761/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "90078902-e1c0-558a-852a-5caf92a1a2ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351761/field-1/1dee3a8b4e2e69484264522b7c3f6207b09ecde7ad469649dd240ad6a8e6346d" :answer-field/choices ["q-351761/field-1/1dee3a8b4e2e69484264522b7c3f6207b09ecde7ad469649dd240ad6a8e6346d" "q-351761/field-1/608b697f811192589eec3729e28e5d056be7cd5127af1a846d717a33508ae203"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351761" :question/id #uuid "e127f88c-db6e-5b23-8bff-fce75b5840ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-351761" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/7151efaca7e4356e590a437f5d50a2ab6e5646e960b7ffa82b0bbed27bb9db47.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-351761/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "808894a0-c611-5eb7-bd57-7f7029f0ef0c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351761"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-152667"] :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={t}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=t\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={t}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=3{\\int }_{\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})]-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/c870dd367332455f2d4c35f85a188c64385a5195ca1058405a93f84c4ce31fd1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})]-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-20502"] :question/problem "Use the substitution $u={e}^{x}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={e}^{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={e}^{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/f55b37b456a6ab60c495c64be5f7afe510dc0889890ed2a070b371f9bbe69c1a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-347151/field-1/ccc5b7f3cfdb4c2dfc0e1f31be54559a1ab3adaf142e500fc1f1b7bc87e93b08" :answer/id #uuid "4e1a182c-d4c7-5630-8e7a-7205a9fb1965" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-347151/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f8d256d-b234-56a2-b411-765b0d92245e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-347151/field-1/ccc5b7f3cfdb4c2dfc0e1f31be54559a1ab3adaf142e500fc1f1b7bc87e93b08" :answer-field/choices ["q-347151/field-1/ccc5b7f3cfdb4c2dfc0e1f31be54559a1ab3adaf142e500fc1f1b7bc87e93b08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-347151" :question/id #uuid "446e9ecd-b592-5a0b-a96b-b10da1e2dac9" :question/math-academy-id "q-347151" :question/problem "Use the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/28afd0a065525cd225377165c2164a1ecc254b1be8aba60d64324d14430c3be4.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-347151/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "808894a0-c611-5eb7-bd57-7f7029f0ef0c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-347151"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10687"] :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac{4}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\arctan(x) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a \\to -\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =4\\cdot (0-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]) \\\\  & =-4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y = \\arctan{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =-4(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10552"] :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1/4}^\\infty \\dfrac {2}{1+16x^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(kx)^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(kx \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]}_{-1/4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan{(4x)},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/9a7272f8bc8d0eba3e5bfc38e843cd02db3f3eb7b51e77b95b2efd61034890b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10551"] :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^\\infty \\dfrac{6}{x^2+4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{k^2 + x^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left( \\dfrac{x}{k} \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]}_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10688"] :question/problem "Use the substitution $u = x^2$ to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int}_{1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{2x}{1 + x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u = x^2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u = x^2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/46e6cf2950c99831a7445a862fb36a6a7b8f9233a82e49c7cf5da4a383ec4756.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
